"[2511.16449v1] VLA-Pruner: Temporal-Aware Dual-Level Visual Token Pruning for Efficient Vision-Language-Action Inference (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16449v1)\nciteturn2483view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.16449v1\",\"lineno\":8}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 26 May 2026 (cite5†v5 )]\nL9: # Title:VLA-Pruner: Temporal-Aware Dual-Level Visual Token Pruning for Efficient Vision-Language-Action Inference\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ziyan Liu , cite7†Yeqiu Chen , cite8†Hongyi Cai , cite9†Tao Lin , cite10†Shuo Yang , cite6†Zheng Liu , cite11†Bo Zhao L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled VLA-Pruner: Temporal-Aware Dual-Level Visual Token Pruning for Efficient Vision-Language-Action Inference, by Ziyan Liu and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have shown great promise for embodied AI, yet the heavy computational cost of processing continuous visual streams severely limits their real-time deployment. Token pruning (keeping salient visual tokens and dropping redundant ones) has emerged as an effective approach for accelerating Vision-Language Models (VLMs), offering a solution for efficient VLA.\nL17: However, these VLM-specific token pruning methods select tokens based solely on semantic salience metrics (e.g., prefill attention), while overlooking the VLA's intrinsic dual-system nature of high-level semantic understanding and low-level action execution. Consequently, these methods bias token retention toward semantic cues, discard critical information for action generation, and significantly degrade VLA performance.\nL18: To bridge this gap, we propose VLA-Pruner, a versatile plug-and-play VLA-specific token prune method that aligns with the dual-system nature of VLA models and exploits the temporal continuity in robot manipulation. Specifically, VLA-Pruner adopts a dual-level importance criterion for visual token retention: vision-language prefill attention for semantic-level relevance and action decode attention, estimated via temporal smoothing, for action-level importance.\nL19: Based on this criterion, VLA-Pruner proposes a novel dual-level token selection strategy that adaptively preserves a compact, informative set of visual tokens for both semantic understanding and action execution under given compute budget. Experiments show that VLA-Pruner achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple VLA architectures and diverse robotic tasks.\nL20: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2511.16449 [cs.CV]\nL22:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2511.16449v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL23:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.16449†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Ziyan Liu [cite17†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Thu, 20 Nov 2025 15:16:09 UTC (1,779 KB)\nL28: cite18†[v2] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 11:57:47 UTC (1,779 KB)\nL29: cite19†[v3] Tue, 10 Feb 2026 05:44:18 UTC (1,786 KB)\nL30: cite20†[v4] Mon, 25 May 2026 17:05:43 UTC (1,763 KB)\nL31: cite5†[v5] Tue, 26 May 2026 14:15:17 UTC (1,758 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: ## Access Paper:\nL35: \nL36: View a PDF of the paper titled VLA-Pruner: Temporal-Aware Dual-Level Visual Token Pruning for Efficient Vision-Language-Action Inference, by Ziyan Liu and 6 other authors\nL37: \nL38:   * cite12†View PDF L39:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L40:   * cite21†